====================================================================================================================
A. the QUMOND phantom density of a pair, on a grid
====================================================================================================================
  resolution matters: the saddle's structure is kpc-scale, so a grid coarser than ~1 kpc misses it.  Scan of box size, 300 kpc pair:
   half-box [kpc]  cell [kpc]   rho_ph(saddle)     min rho_ph  negative fraction
            150.0       2.500        1.313e+05     -4.449e+04              3.36%
             30.0       0.500        2.940e+05     -1.010e+05             25.56%
              5.0       0.083        7.202e+05     -2.474e+05             27.01%
              1.0       0.017        1.610e+06     -5.533e+05             27.08%
  canonical  pair of 5e+10 Msun at 300 kpc (r_M = 8.6 kpc): on the fine grid the phantom density reaches -2.474e+05 Msun/kpc^3 and is NEGATIVE over 27% of the saddle region
  alt        pair of 5e+10 Msun at 300 kpc (r_M = 7.9 kpc): on the fine grid the phantom density reaches -2.719e+05 Msun/kpc^3 and is NEGATIVE over 27% of the saddle region
  [PASS] 71a (CONFIRMED, once the saddle is resolved) the QUMOND phantom density DOES go negative around the saddle between two galaxies -- negative mass, which no arrangement of dark matter produces -- over about a quarter of the saddle region, on both footings.  A grid coarser than ~1 kpc misses it entirely, which is why the first run of this script reported none   (min rho_ph = -2.474e+05 Msun/kpc^3, negative over 27% of a +-5 kpc box; at +-150 kpc resolution only 3.4% is negative)

====================================================================================================================
B. the observable: projected surface density through the midpoint versus two isolated lenses
====================================================================================================================
  separation   200 kpc (23.18 r_M): Sigma_ph(midpoint, pair) = +9.011e+06, sum of two isolated = +2.693e+07 Msun/kpc^2, ratio = +0.335
  separation   300 kpc (34.76 r_M): Sigma_ph(midpoint, pair) = +5.990e+06, sum of two isolated = +1.794e+07 Msun/kpc^2, ratio = +0.334
  separation   500 kpc (57.94 r_M): Sigma_ph(midpoint, pair) = +3.587e+06, sum of two isolated = +1.076e+07 Msun/kpc^2, ratio = +0.333
  separation   800 kpc (92.70 r_M): Sigma_ph(midpoint, pair) = +2.239e+06, sum of two isolated = +6.722e+06 Msun/kpc^2, ratio = +0.333
  [PASS] 71b (the prediction, quantified for the first time here) the projected phantom surface density at the midpoint of a galaxy pair is SUPPRESSED relative to the sum of two isolated lenses -- a deficit, not an excess, which no dark-matter arrangement produces   (at 300 kpc (34.76 MOND radii): pair +5.990e+06 vs sum-of-isolated +1.794e+07 Msun/kpc^2, ratio 0.334 -- a 67% deficit)

====================================================================================================================
C. is either effect measurable?
====================================================================================================================
  TWO separate effects, and only one of them is large:
    (i) the NEGATIVE-density region at the saddle is only a few kpc across -- its projected mass is ~1e5 Msun/kpc^3 x (few kpc),
        i.e. under 1 Msun/pc^2 spread over an aperture of a few arcseconds.  Undetectable, and it is not what the ratio in B measures.
    (ii) the large-scale DEFICIT in B is the nonlinearity of MOND itself: the phantom halo of a PAIR is not the sum of two phantom
        halos, and at the midpoint it is only a third of that sum.  That is a 67% suppression, far bigger than the 10-30% guessed
        when this item was written -- but the projected Sigma is logarithmically box-dependent for a 1/r^2 phantom, so the ABSOLUTE
        surface density below is indicative and the RATIO is the robust number.
  indicative projected deficit at the midpoint (box +-1800 kpc): 11.95 Msun/pc^2 against a typical Sigma_crit of ~3000
  -> delta kappa ~ 4.0e-03 per pair, versus a per-galaxy shape-noise sigma_kappa of ~0.3 in a 15-arcsec aperture
  [PASS] 71c the effect that matters is the MOND nonlinearity, not the negative saddle: a 67% suppression of the phantom surface density between paired galaxies relative to two isolated ones.  The indicative aperture estimate says a 3-sigma stack needs of order 1e3-1e4 pairs, which KiDS and DES already have -- but the absolute normalisation here is box-dependent, so this is a GO-LOOK, not a forecast   (ratio 0.334 (a 67% deficit, stable across 200-800 kpc separations); indicative delta kappa 4.0e-03, N_pairs ~ 6.3e+02 for 3 sigma)
  what a real forecast needs, and this script does NOT do: the differential surface density Delta Sigma rather than Sigma, a
  finite projection depth matched to the survey's own radial binning, realistic unequal masses, and the neighbour/two-halo term
  that a dark-matter universe puts in the same place.  Those are a week's work and they are the right next step for this item.

====================================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================================
  Item 71 survives its first calculation and gets BIGGER, not smaller.  The negative-mass region at the saddle is real and
  resolved -- and irrelevant, being a few kpc across.  What matters is the nonlinearity: the phantom halo of a galaxy PAIR is
  only a third of the sum of two isolated phantom halos at the midpoint, a 67% deficit stable from 200 to 800 kpc separation,
  and no dark-matter arrangement makes a deficit at all -- overlapping halos and the shared filament both ADD.  The indicative
  aperture estimate puts a 3-sigma stack within reach of KiDS or DES, which would make this the sharpest MOND-versus-halo test
  available on existing data.  Reported honestly: the absolute normalisation here is box-dependent and the forecast is a
  go-look, not a number to quote.  Item 71 is PROMOTED to the top of the priority list.

